<span class="kicker">2026-07-29 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How Thick Should Paver Base Be for a Walkway</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>The base for a paver walkway should generally be 4 to 6 inches of compacted gravel, plus about 1 inch of bedding sand on top, bringing the total sub-paver depth to roughly 5 to 7 inches before the pavers themselves go down. That range holds for most well-drained soil, but it shifts depending on your soil type, and it’s noticeably less than what a patio or driveway needs, since a walkway carries far less weight.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, base depth is one of the first things we assess on every walkway project across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, since it’s the single factor that determines whether a walkway holds up for decades or starts shifting within a year or two. This guide breaks down exactly how thick your base should be, how soil type changes that number, and how walkways compare to patios and driveways in this regard.</p>
<h2><b>Standard Paver Walkway Base Thickness at a Glance</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Layer</b></td><td><b>Typical Depth</b></td><td><b>Purpose</b></td></tr><tr><td>Compacted gravel base</td><td>4 to 6 inches</td><td>Provides structural support and drainage</td></tr><tr><td>Bedding sand</td><td>1 inch</td><td>Creates a level surface for pavers to sit on</td></tr><tr><td>Pavers</td><td>1.5 to 2.5 inches (varies by product)</td><td>Walking surface</td></tr><tr><td>Total sub-paver depth</td><td>5 to 7 inches</td><td>Combined gravel and sand before pavers</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This is a starting point rather than a fixed number for every yard. Soil conditions, drainage, and how the walkway will actually be used all push this number up or down.</p>
<h2><b>Layer by Layer: What Each Part of the Base Actually Does</b></h2>
<h3><b>Compacted Gravel Base</b></h3>
<p>This is the structural layer doing most of the work. Crushed gravel or road base, compacted in layers rather than all at once, distributes weight evenly and allows water to drain through rather than pooling under the pavers. Skipping proper compaction here, even with the right depth of material, is one of the most common reasons walkways settle unevenly within the first year.</p>
<h3><b>Bedding Sand</b></h3>
<p>A thin, roughly 1 inch layer of coarse sand sits on top of the compacted gravel, providing a level, adjustable surface for pavers to be set into. This layer is not meant to add structural strength, that’s the gravel’s job, it’s purely there to allow for fine leveling adjustments as pavers are placed.</p>
<h3><b>The Pavers Themselves</b></h3>
<p>Paver thickness varies by product, typically between 1.5 and 2.5 inches for standard concrete pavers used on walkways. This adds to the overall height of the finished walkway but isn’t part of the base thickness conversation in the same way the gravel and sand layers are, since the paver itself doesn’t compact or settle the way base materials do.</p>
<h2><b>How Base Thickness Changes by Soil Type</b></h2>
<h3><b>Sandy or Well-Drained Soil</b></h3>
<p>Sandy soil drains well naturally, which means it doesn’t hold as much water against the base during freeze and thaw cycles. A standard 4 inch gravel base is often sufficient in these conditions, since the soil itself isn’t adding much additional moisture-related movement.</p>
<h3><b>Loamy or Average Garden Soil</b></h3>
<p>Most residential yards fall somewhere in this middle range, soil that drains reasonably well but isn’t as loose as pure sand. A base depth of 4 to 5 inches typically works well here, matching the general standard range without needing to push toward the upper end.</p>
<h3><b>Heavy Clay Soil</b></h3>
<p>Clay soil holds water and expands significantly during freeze and thaw cycles, which puts more pressure on the base and any structure sitting above it. In clay-heavy yards, common across parts of the Denver area, we typically recommend pushing base depth to the full 6 inches, sometimes slightly more, to give the walkway enough of a buffer against that seasonal ground movement.</p>
<h3><b>Poor Drainage or Consistently Wet Areas</b></h3>
<p>If a section of yard tends to stay damp or hold standing water after rain, even briefly, the base underneath a walkway there needs extra attention beyond just adding depth. In these spots, it’s often worth adding a layer of larger, more open-graded stone at the very bottom of the excavation before the standard compacted gravel base, to help water move through rather than sitting against the walkway’s foundation. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-paver-walkway-on-a-slope/"> how to build a paver walkway on a slope</a> covers a related situation, where water movement through the base becomes a central design consideration rather than an afterthought.</p>
<h2><b>How Walkway Base Depth Compares to Patios and Driveways</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Application</b></td><td><b>Typical Base Depth</b></td><td><b>Why It Differs</b></td></tr><tr><td>Walkway</td><td>4 to 6 inches</td><td>Foot traffic only, minimal weight load</td></tr><tr><td>Patio</td><td>4 to 6 inches</td><td>Similar to walkways, occasional furniture weight</td></tr><tr><td>Driveway</td><td>8 to 12 inches</td><td>Must support vehicle weight, repeated heavy loads</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Walkways and patios generally use similar base depths, since both are designed for foot traffic and light furniture rather than vehicles. Driveways need a noticeably deeper base, often double a walkway’s depth, because they have to support the sustained weight of vehicles without the base compressing or shifting under that load over time. If a walkway will ever see occasional vehicle traffic, for example, a path that doubles as an overflow parking area, it needs to be built closer to driveway specifications rather than standard walkway depth. Our guide on<a href="/blog/paver-base-depth-for-patios-proper-installation-tips/"> proper paver base depth for patios</a> covers this topic in more detail for patio-specific applications, which largely mirrors what’s needed for a standard walkway.</p>

